<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So really what I am looking for in this section is more of an overview of the article: what's the narrative being included? What do you do with this SCP beyond just describing all the parts? Like how are you really going to show the horror of this in a way that actually scares a reader? Are you having exploration logs or incident reports or what?</p>
<p>My big concern with this is that the idea is extremely complex: there's like, 8 different things that are going on here (the long teeth/claws, the spine as a tail to attract mates, can't leave the cornfield, die if fed the wrong thing etc) and it just&#8230;feels unnecessary? Like it makes this feel a lot more like an entry in a DnD Monster Manual than it does a short story about a scary monster, particularly since you don't actually have any kinda&#8230;.indication of scenes involving said monster being scary, y'know?</p>
<p>Like, Alien or The Thing works because there's contrast: there's opening scenes setting up the characters, letting us know who they are and what they're like, followed by scenes in which they get glimpses of the monster and gradually like, get killed off and stuff, and then an ending where they resolve the tension of the monster somehow; and this feels like all it has is the monster? But a monster isn't scary in isolation: it's scary because of what it does and <em>to who</em>.</p>
</div>
