<blockquote>
<p>What is the reason in the Foundation universe world for the radio to do what it does? Did someone make it because they wanted to talk to their grandma? Is a ghost grandma living in the radiowaves to haunt them? Some other reason?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Oh yeah, here is what I am trying to imply in the story; a mysterious benevolent entity/company is trying to help humanity regain contact with their dead loved ones, in this case, grandmas. How? by turning previously non-anomalous radios into a medium of such conversations with said individuals, though it is still unsure whether they are the actual person.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>My point is that this is a kind of boring initial investigation setup.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Okay, I respect your opinion. Though I still like to point out that some articles do have a similar setup as mine, which I took the inspiration from.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Why would the creator want to play games with the Foundation?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A part of the mystery I would also like to imply with the readers. Also, I planned this as an initial setup for future articles of mine.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm not familiar with antique radios. Although, if it only affects such radios, would there really be that many people who would be affected by such an anomaly, to the point where the Foundation would have enough cases to notice?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yep.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I personally am not convinced you'd be able to make a tale for this—the material I've mentioned would make more sense for a log or supplement page. Focus on writing an SCP article first.</p>
</blockquote>
<ul>
<li>Oh yeah, that's the right word I am looking for. A tale in a format of what you've said, my bad.</li>
