			<div class="content" id="post-content-4843704">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The range of its search doubles every 4 hours that it doesn't have someone to heal and it gets more and more agitated that it can't find someone to heal.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How does this 'searching' work? Like is it physically going to places? Does it just, radar sweep for people? Why does the range increase exponentially like this?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After 24 hours it will attempt to heal terminal medical conditions, resulting in accidental euthanasia.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>how does this result in accidental euthanasia? This feels like you're going for like 'too much healing is bad' or something but it's not exactly clear how it gets here since the implication is the mannequin is good at healing? This applies to the 'healed of humanity' angle too like&#8230;there's not really any indication the mannequin heals badly until it gets too big?</p>
<hr />
<p>I think my big issue with this is that&#8230;it just, doesn't feel very interesting to me? There's a lot of elements that are here but don't really go anywhere, or just feel added in to add more threat to this (e.g., the 'gk world ending scenario' angle, the 'aggression' of the SCP, the exponentially increasing range), and it feels like focussing on this being a SCP that can heal as the only thing it does would be more effective, particularly since the core of the story you want to tell is 'foundation tries to stop it healing and it doesn't'.</p>
<p>Part of the problem with this though is it feels&#8230;very surface level? You don't really do anything with this beyond having it heal someone, and I'm just kinda&#8230;looking for more emotional impact here? Like, some kind of exploration of its existence that makes me feel sorry for it not helping, or like, really ties the 'overhealing' kinda stuff in so it feels like an important part of the narrative or something, y'know?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

